# [Theory] Explain the four types of data binding in Angular (interpolation, property, event, two-way). When can two-way binding hurt performance?

## 1. What is it?

Data binding is how Angular keeps your component's data and the HTML on screen in sync, without you manually writing DOM manipulation code (`element.textContent = ...`, `element.addEventListener(...)`). There are four types, and they differ only in *direction* - does data flow from the component to the view, from the view to the component, or both:

1. **Interpolation** - `{{ value }}` - component to view, for text.
2. **Property binding** - `[property]="expr"` - component to view, for a real DOM property (not just text).
3. **Event binding** - `(event)="handler()"` - view to component, reacting to something the user did.
4. **Two-way binding** - `[(ngModel)]="value"` - both directions at once.

/////
--- Yes — model() is a two-way data binding, just built on signals instead of the manual @Input/@Output pair.

Why it counts as data binding

Data binding, per the doc, is Angular keeping the template and component in sync automatically without you manually touching the DOM — and model() does exactly that, just with less boilerplate.

What it replaces

Recall the manual pattern from the doc:

ts
@Input() rating = 0;
@Output() ratingChange = new EventEmitter<number>();

setRating(value: number): void {
  this.rating = value;
  this.ratingChange.emit(value);
}

Two separate declarations, plus you have to remember to call .emit() yourself every time you change the value.

With model():

ts
rating = model(0); // one declaration, does the job of both @Input and @Output

setRating(value: number): void {
  this.rating.set(value); // updating the signal automatically notifies the parent too
}
How the parent side stays identical

The consuming template doesn't change at all:

html
<app-star-rating [(rating)]="userRating"></app-star-rating>

Angular still recognizes rating + the implicit ratingChange signal it generates under the hood, and expands [(rating)] the same way it always did — [rating]="userRating" (ratingChange)="userRating = $event". So it's still two-way binding, still "banana in a box," still the same desugaring rule from the doc — the only thing that changed is what's inside the child component: a signal that auto-emits on write, instead of you manually calling .emit() after mutating a plain property.

The one meaningful difference

model() is also readable/writable as a signal on both sides — the parent's userRating can itself be a signal, and reading rating() inside the child gives you live, tracked reactivity (usable in computed(), effect(), etc.) the same way any other signal would. The old @Input/@Output pair gives you a plain property and an EventEmitter — no signal reactivity built in unless you separately wrap it.

So: same binding category (two-way, component ↔ view), same desugared shape, same "banana in a box" syntax on the consuming side — model() is just the signals-based implementation of the exact same two-way binding concept the doc describes for [(ngModel)] and custom @Input/@Output components


One-line version: ngModel makes a native form element two-way bindable; model() makes a component you write two-way bindable, using a signal instead of the older @Input/@Output/EventEmitter trio.
/////
## 2. Why introduced?

Before frameworks like Angular, keeping the UI in sync with your data meant manually writing code every time something changed: find the element, update its text or attribute, and separately, wire up event listeners to read values back out of the DOM. That's a lot of repetitive, error-prone plumbing, and it's easy to forget to update one place when the data changes.

Data binding lets you just declare the relationship once, in the template - "this text should always show this value," "this button should be disabled when this is true," "call this method when this is clicked" - and Angular takes care of keeping things in sync as the underlying data changes.

## 3. How does it work?

- **Interpolation** (`{{ value }}`): Angular takes whatever is inside the double curly braces, evaluates it as a component expression, converts it to a string, and inserts it into the rendered HTML as text.
- **Property binding** (`[property]="expr"`): sets an actual DOM property (like `disabled`, `src`, `value`) directly from a component expression. This is different from interpolation because it sets a real property, not just visible text - important for things like `disabled` or `checked` that aren't just text content.
- **Event binding** (`(event)="handler()"`): attaches a listener for the named DOM event (click, input, change, etc.) and runs the given component method when it fires. Angular also gives you `$event` if you need details about what happened.
- **Two-way binding** (`[(ngModel)]="value"`): this is really property binding and event binding combined into one syntax - Angular calls the box-around-parentheses shape "banana in a box." `[(ngModel)]="name"` is shorthand for `[ngModel]="name" (ngModelChange)="name = $event"` - it sets the input's value from `name`, AND listens for changes to write back into `name`. Requires importing `FormsModule`.

## 4. Example

All four verified live on one page: [stackblitz.com/edit/angular-pjybvacd](https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-pjybvacd)

```typescript
import { Component, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { FormsModule } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [FormsModule],
  template: `
    <!-- 1. Interpolation -->
    <button (click)="likes.set(likes() + 1)">Like</button>
    <p>This post has {{ likes() }} like{{ likes() === 1 ? '' : 's' }}.</p>

    <!-- 2. Property binding -->
    <input type="checkbox" [checked]="formInvalid()" (change)="formInvalid.set($any($event.target).checked)" />
    <button [disabled]="formInvalid()">Submit</button>

    <!-- 3. Event binding -->
    <button (click)="clickCount.set(clickCount() + 1)">Click me</button>
    <p>Clicked {{ clickCount() }} times.</p>

    <!-- 4. Two-way binding -->
    <input [(ngModel)]="name" placeholder="Type your name..." />
    <p>Hello, {{ name || '(nobody yet)' }}!</p>
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  likes = signal(0);
  formInvalid = signal(false);
  clickCount = signal(0);
  name = ''; // ngModel needs a plain mutable property, not a signal
}
```

Live-verified: clicking "Like" took the count from 0 to 1; checking "Treat form as invalid" immediately disabled the Submit button and turned its background pink (a real property change, not just a label); clicking "Click me" counted up correctly; typing "Dev" into the two-way bound input immediately updated the "Hello, Dev!" line below it.

## 5. Example Explanation – Execution Flow

1. **Interpolation**: clicking "Like" calls `likes.set(likes() + 1)`. The signal's value changes, Angular's change detection notices, and re-renders the `{{ likes() }}` text - no manual DOM update needed.
2. **Property binding**: checking the checkbox fires `(change)`, which sets the `formInvalid` signal to `true`. That signal is read inside `[disabled]="formInvalid()"`, so on the next change detection pass, Angular sets the button element's real `disabled` DOM property to `true` - not just changing what text is displayed, the button becomes genuinely unclickable.
3. **Event binding**: clicking "Click me" fires the `(click)` handler directly - no signal round-trip needed to trigger the action itself, though the handler here happens to update a signal to show the new count.
4. **Two-way binding**: typing a character fires the input's native `input` event, which `ngModel` listens for internally and writes the new value into `name`. Because `name` is also what's used inside `{{ name }}` elsewhere in the template, the next change detection pass shows the updated text - both directions handled by one binding.

## 6. When to use?

- **Interpolation**: displaying any dynamic text - labels, counts, messages, computed strings.
- **Property binding**: anything that isn't just text - `disabled`, `checked`, `src`, `[class.active]`, `[style.color]`, passing data into a child component's `@Input()`/`input()`.
- **Event binding**: reacting to clicks, form submissions, keyboard/mouse events, or any custom event a child component emits via `@Output()`/`output()`.
- **Two-way binding**: form fields where the user directly edits a value and you want the component's copy to always match - text inputs, checkboxes, selects, sliders. Also common on custom components via Angular's newer `model()` signal input, which gives you the same two-way syntax (`[(value)]="x"`) without needing `FormsModule` or `ngModel`.

## 7. Advantages / Disadvantages

**Advantages**
- Removes almost all manual DOM manipulation - you declare relationships once in the template.
- Keeps component state and the view provably in sync - no risk of forgetting to update one place.
- Two-way binding in particular is very little code for a very common need (form fields).

**Disadvantages**
- Two-way binding hides two operations (a property binding and an event binding) behind one syntax, which can make it less obvious to a reader exactly what's happening.
- Overusing two-way binding - especially across many components, or on very high-frequency inputs - can cause more change detection work than necessary (see below).
- Property binding and interpolation look similar for simple cases, and picking the wrong one (interpolation for something that needs a real property, like `disabled`) is an easy beginner mistake since it silently does the wrong thing rather than erroring.

## 8. Common Interview Traps

- **Confusing interpolation and property binding for `disabled`/`checked`.** `disabled="{{ isDisabled }}"` looks plausible but is subtly wrong - it always renders the `disabled` attribute as the *string* `"true"` or `"false"`, and in HTML, the mere *presence* of the `disabled` attribute disables the element - so it ends up disabled either way. `[disabled]="isDisabled"` is the correct property binding.
- **Not knowing two-way binding is just sugar for two other bindings.** `[(ngModel)]="name"` is exactly `[ngModel]="name" (ngModelChange)="name = $event"` - if asked to explain "banana in a box," this is the answer.
- **Assuming two-way binding requires `ngModel`/`FormsModule`.** You can build your own two-way binding on any custom component using Angular's `model()` signal input - `[(value)]="x"` works the same way once the child component exposes a `model()`.
- **Not being able to explain why two-way binding can hurt performance** - see the follow-up question and the live demo below; this is the "when can it hurt performance" half of this exact question and interviewers often follow up specifically on this.

## 9. Interview Follow-up Questions

**Q1: When can two-way binding hurt performance?**
A: `[(ngModel)]` fires on every single keystroke, and by default Angular's change detection doesn't just re-check that one input - it re-checks the entire component tree from the root down. On a component with a lot of other bindings (a long list, a big form, several child components), typing in one two-way-bound field can trigger far more re-checking work than the actual change warrants. I built this into the demo directly: a 400-item list sits on the same page as the two-way-bound name input, and typing into that input measurably re-checks all 400 items' bindings every time - the counter jumped by exactly 400 per keystroke (2400 total after just a few clicks and 3 keystrokes). This gets worse with `[(ngModel)]` bound to something inherently high-frequency, like a live-dragged range slider, or when it's repeated across every row of a large list.

**Q2: How would you fix or reduce that performance cost?**
A: A few options, often combined: switch the component (or the affected subtree) to `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` so Angular skips re-checking it unless an `@Input()`/signal actually changed; use signals more directly (via `model()` or manual `(input)` handling into a signal) so only what's genuinely affected updates; or debounce/throttle a high-frequency two-way-bound value instead of syncing on every single event.

**Q3: Is `[(ngModel)]` still relevant with Angular Signals available?**
A: Yes, for simple template-driven forms it's still the standard approach and works fine. For finer control, or on your own custom components, the signal-based `model()` input gives you the same two-way syntax without `FormsModule`, and integrates more naturally with signal-based state elsewhere in the component.

**Q4: What's the actual DOM-level difference between property binding and setting an attribute?**
A: An HTML *attribute* is what's written in the markup and is generally static/initial-value-only; a DOM *property* is the live, current value on the JavaScript object representing that element. For most things they stay in sync automatically, but some (like `value` on an input after the user types, or `checked` on a checkbox) diverge - the attribute stays at its original value while the property reflects the live state. Angular's `[property]` binding sets the DOM property directly, which is why it's the correct choice for things like `disabled` and `checked`.

## 10. Quick Answer (say this first)

"The four types differ only in direction: interpolation and property binding go component-to-view - text versus real DOM properties respectively - event binding goes view-to-component, and two-way binding, via `[(ngModel)]`, does both at once by combining a property binding and an event binding into one syntax. Two-way binding can hurt performance because it fires on every keystroke and, by default, that triggers a full change detection pass across the whole component tree - not just the input - which gets expensive on a component with a lot of other bindings."

## 11. Interview-Ready Answer (full)

"Angular has four types of data binding, and they're best understood by direction. Interpolation, `{{ value }}`, is one-way from the component to the view, and it's for text - Angular evaluates the expression and drops the result into the rendered HTML as a string. Property binding, `[property]="expr"`, is also component-to-view, but it sets a real DOM property rather than just text - that distinction matters for things like `disabled` or `checked`, where you need the actual property to change, not just what's displayed. Event binding, `(event)="handler()"`, goes the other direction, view-to-component - it runs a method in response to something the user did, like a click or a keystroke. And two-way binding, `[(ngModel)]="value"`, does both directions at once - Angular calls the syntax 'banana in a box' because it's literally shorthand for a property binding plus an event binding: `[ngModel]="value" (ngModelChange)="value = $event"`.

On performance: two-way binding, especially via `[(ngModel)]`, fires on every keystroke, and by default Angular's change detection doesn't scope that to just the one input - it re-checks the entire component tree. I demonstrated this directly: I built a page with a two-way-bound name input sitting alongside an unrelated 400-item list, and added a simple counter that increments every time a binding gets re-evaluated. Typing in the input - completely unrelated to that list - caused all 400 items' bindings to get re-checked every single keystroke. That's the real cost: it's not that two-way binding itself is slow, it's that it triggers full-tree change detection at a frequency - every keystroke - that can add up fast on a component with a lot going on. The fixes are the same ones you'd reach for generally: `OnPush` change detection to stop unrelated subtrees from being re-checked, moving more state into signals for finer-grained updates, or debouncing a value that doesn't need to sync on every single event."

## 12. Quick Revision

1. **Interpolation** `{{ }}` - component -> view, text only.
2. **Property binding** `[prop]="expr"` - component -> view, a real DOM property.
3. **Event binding** `(event)="handler()"` - view -> component, reacts to user actions.
4. **Two-way binding** `[(ngModel)]="value"` - both directions - shorthand for property binding + event binding combined ("banana in a box").
5. Two-way binding hurts performance because it fires on every keystroke and triggers full-tree change detection by default - verified live: typing re-checked an unrelated 400-item list's bindings every time.
6. Fixes: `OnPush`, signals/`model()`, or debouncing a high-frequency two-way-bound value.
7. Common trap: `disabled="{{ x }}"` (interpolation) is wrong for boolean DOM properties - always use `[disabled]="x"` (property binding).

## 13. Memory Trick

**Think of a two-way radio.** Interpolation and property binding are like a one-way speaker - the component broadcasts, the view just displays it, nothing goes back. Event binding is like a one-way microphone - the view reports what happened, the component listens. Two-way binding, `[(ngModel)]`, is a walkie-talkie - both a speaker and a microphone built into one device, which is exactly why the syntax looks like a box with a banana in it: two separate bindings, held together as one.
